- 2026-07-20: Phase 2 (zones & geofencing) done on `feat/zones-geofencing-phase2`, all 6 tasks reviewed individually. Built: a pure-Python point-in-polygon geofencing utility (`app/core/geo.py`, ray-casting, GeoJSON-aware wrapper, reusable by a future visit check-in feature), a `require_manager` auth dependency, `Zone`/`ZoneAssignment` models + migration (GeoJSON polygon boundary as a plain JSON column, tenant-scoped, unique per rep+zone), and manager-only CRUD/assignment endpoints. Architectural decision: zone reads rely on the global tenancy filter (proven live since Phase 1's final-review fix) instead of redundant explicit `tenant_id` filtering, since every zone endpoint requires authentication first. Per-task review caught two real gaps: `assign_rep_to_zone` had no defense-in-depth check that the looked-up zone's tenant actually matched the caller's tenant (not exploitable via the one real call path, but untested and unguarded against a future mismatched caller) - fixed with a guard clause + two tests that isolate the pre-existing null-check path from the new guard path; and a coordinate-order documentation/test gap in the geofencing utility. Final whole-branch review caught two more: duplicate zone assignment returned an unhandled 500 instead of a clean 409 (API-reachable, reproduced live), and the tenant guard was asymmetric (explicit for the zone, only implicit for the rep) - both fixed and re-verified with mutation testing. Merged to `main` (86/86 tests passing, CI green).
- 2026-07-20: Phase 3 (location pings) done on `feat/location-pings-phase3`, all 6 tasks reviewed individually. Built: a `require_rep` auth dependency (mirrors `require_manager`), `LocationPing` model + migration (client-supplied `recorded_at` distinct from server `created_at`, both timezone-aware), an idempotent `record_ping` service (get-or-create keyed on tenant+rep+idempotency_key, so a tablet's offline-queue retry can never double-count a fix), `prune_old_pings` retention logic (pruning only - downsampling deferred, nothing reads historical pings yet), a rep-only ping ingestion router, and - `job_runner`'s first real consumer - the retention job wired into a new FastAPI `lifespan` handler. Per-task review caught real gaps: `prune_old_pings` had no guard against ambient tenant context leaking in (proven empirically to silently mis-scope the delete to one tenant); `record_ping`'s check-then-insert had no race handling for concurrent identical replays; and - the most structurally interesting one - the ping router's own pre-check for the 201-vs-200 decision raced against `record_ping`'s internal get-or-create, so a concurrent replay's "loser" could report 201 even though its body was the winner's row. That last one was a flaw in the plan's own design, not an implementer mistake - fixed by moving the created-vs-found determination inside `record_ping` itself (returns `(ping, created)` now), which also incidentally restored the thin-router convention. The retention-job-wiring task's reviewer also empirically verified (via standalone diagnostic scripts, not just reasoning) two proactively-flagged concerns: no asyncio task leakage under the test harness, and correct contextvars isolation for the periodic job's tenant-context assert. 104/104 tests passing, ruff clean. Merged to `main` (106/106 tests passing after the JobRunner reliability fix, CI green).
- 2026-07-20: Visits (PLAN 4.1) done on `feat/visits-phase4a`, all 4 tasks reviewed individually. Built: check-in with geofence verification (composes Phase 2's `point_in_geojson_polygon` and `zone_assignments` for the first time), remote/off-site check-in support (optional `zone_id`), check-out, and a rep/manager visibility split on list/get (reps see their own visits, managers see the whole tenant). Photo upload deliberately deferred - schema-ready `photo_path` column only, no storage backend chosen yet. Per-task review caught one real gap the implementer found themselves and reported honestly rather than shipping quietly: the geofence tests all used symmetric lat==lon coordinates against a square boundary, so none of them could actually discriminate a `(lon,lat)` vs `(lat,lon)` bug at the `check_in` call site - the exact bug class Phase 2 already caught once at the utility-function layer, recurring one layer up. Fixed with an asymmetric-rectangle test, proven load-bearing via swap-and-revert (independently reproduced by the reviewer in an isolated scratch copy). 135/135 tests passing, ruff clean. Next: final whole-branch review, then merge, then plan tasks (PLAN 4.2) fresh against the actual code.
- 2026-07-20: Tasks (PLAN 4.2) done on `feat/tasks-phase4b`, all 4 tasks reviewed individually, this completes Phase 4. Built: `Task` model with a three-state OPEN/COMPLETED/CANCELLED lifecycle (cancellation is the soft-delete equivalent of "CRUD" - no literal DELETE endpoint), manager-assigns-to-rep creation reusing Phase 2's `RepNotFoundError` (rep-lookup query has an explicit tenant check, same defense-in-depth precedent as `assign_rep_to_zone`), due dates and LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH priority, complete/cancel/update guarded by a generic not-OPEN check, and a rep/manager visibility split on list/get - all built with pagination from the first commit instead of needing a final-review fix-round like visits did. Status/priority are plain unconstrained `String` columns (matches `User.role`'s existing precedent, Pydantic-layer validation only), verified against the real `auth/models.py` file rather than assumed. Per-task reviews caught nothing Critical/Important; one Minor finding carried to the final review (no test for the terminal-state guard against an already-CANCELLED task, only already-COMPLETED) was judged ship-as-is since the guard is a single generic branch already proven by the COMPLETED case. Final whole-branch review re-verified the tenancy filter and all five routers are still wired into `main.py`, both new datetime columns are `DateTime(timezone=True)` in the actual migration, and traced cross-tenant/cross-rep isolation per endpoint against the real code - READY TO MERGE, no blocking findings. 174/174 tests passing, ruff clean. This completes Phase 4 (visits & tasks) in full. Next: plan Phase 5 (goals).
- 2026-07-20: Goals (PLAN 5.1) done on `feat/goals-phase5`, all 4 tasks reviewed individually. Built: `Goal` model (VISIT_COUNT/TASK_COUNT/SALES_VALUE metric types, plain-`String` per the `User.role`/`Task.status` precedent), manager-creates-for-rep with the same explicit-tenant-check pattern as tasks/zones, a target-vs-achievement progress endpoint that computes VISIT_COUNT/TASK_COUNT by counting `visits`/`tasks` rows in a date-derived range (SALES_VALUE has no live source per design.md, so it's a rep-logged manual value instead). Scope cut: goals are per-rep only for v1 - no `teams` concept exists anywhere in this codebase, so "rep or team" from design.md's goals row is narrowed to rep-only until a real team entity exists elsewhere. The one genuinely new risk this feature introduced - converting a `Date` period into a tz-aware `DateTime` range for counting - was caught the first time: a half-open-range helper (`[period_start, period_end+1day)`) plus an asymmetric boundary test (a row at 23:59:59 on the last day included, one at 00:00:00 the next day excluded), mirroring the discipline visits/zones needed for their own coordinate-order and timezone bugs. Per-task review also caught and fixed a real latent bug in the plan itself - two tests hardcoded goal periods to a fixed future month while asserting against rows stamped with real wall-clock `datetime.now(utc)`, a time-bomb that would've broken depending on what day the suite ran; fixed by bracketing "today" dynamically instead, verified not to weaken the boundary test's own coverage (that one already used fixed dates via direct row construction, untouched). Final whole-branch review re-verified the tenancy filter and all six routers wired into `main.py`, the migration's timezone column, cross-tenant/cross-rep isolation per endpoint, and re-derived the boundary math from first principles independently - READY TO MERGE. One Minor finding (no cross-tenant test on the `log_value` mutation path, though the guard itself mirrors the already-tested `complete_task`/`check_out` pattern) shipped as known-acceptable. 204/204 tests passing, ruff clean. This completes Phase 5 (goals) in full. Next: plan Phase 6 (violations & alerts).
- 2026-07-20: Violations & alerts (PLAN 6.1/6.2) done on `feat/violations-alerts-phase6`, all 5 tasks reviewed individually, this completes Phase 6. Built: work-hours-gated (fixed 08:00-18:00 UTC) zone-violation detection wired into ping ingestion (`evaluate_ping`, the third call site in this codebase's history to get `point_in_geojson_polygon`'s lng/lat order right, with its own asymmetric boundary test), an open/resolved violation lifecycle capped at one open violation per rep, and VAPID web push via `pywebpush` to subscribed managers on new detection only. Two deliberate design decisions worth remembering: work-hours gates *detecting* a new violation but never gates *resolving* one (a rep returning after hours still closes out an open violation), and `event_bus.publish("ZoneViolationDetected", ...)` documents the domain event per design.md but push-sending is wired as a direct awaited call from the router rather than a bus-subscribed handler - the bus's `publish`/`subscribe` are synchronous and bridging into async push-sending through it would need fire-and-forget `asyncio.create_task` with its own GC/exception-swallowing risk that a plain awaited call sidesteps entirely. Per-task review caught two real gaps during Task 2: the plan's own literal code called `db.refresh()` after commit, which on SQLite silently strips tzinfo off `DateTime(timezone=True)` columns on re-select (a genuine SQLite/aiosqlite quirk, reproduced independently by the reviewer in isolation, and confirmed load-bearing by restoring the removed calls and reproducing the identical two test failures) - fixed by dropping the refresh calls, safe here since every field is client-set. Final whole-branch review caught one real, previously-missed bug: `send_zone_violation_push` only caught `WebPushException`, but `pywebpush.webpush()` only raises that for HTTP-status failures - a network-level failure (`requests.exceptions.ConnectionError`, timeouts, SSL errors) would propagate unwrapped, aborting the notify loop for any managers after the failing one AND 500ing the rep's ping request even though the ping and violation were already committed. Fixed by broadening the catch to `(WebPushException, requests.exceptions.RequestException)`, re-reviewed clean. Three Minor findings shipped as known-acceptable: a TOCTOU race on the at-most-one-open-violation invariant (no DB-level partial unique index; fast-follow), `_is_within_work_hours` not explicitly normalizing to UTC (safe today since `recorded_at` always arrives UTC-aware via the DB round-trip), and no dedicated two-tenant isolation test for the two new endpoint families (code traced correct by the final reviewer, coverage gap only). 234/234 tests passing, ruff clean. This completes Phase 6 (violations & alerts) in full. Next: plan Phase 7 (manager web dashboard).
- 2026-07-20: Dashboard backend prerequisites done on `feat/dashboard-backend-prereqs`, not its own PLAN.md line item - four gaps discovered while designing Phase 7 (manager web dashboard) that nothing in Phases 1-6 needed: `GET /api/v1/users` (optional role filter, for assignment pickers and resolving rep_id to a name), `GET /api/v1/zones/{zone_id}/assignments` (who's assigned to a zone), `GET /api/v1/push-subscriptions/vapid-public-key` (the frontend needs this to construct a browser push subscription), and `GET /api/v1/pings/latest` (one row per rep, for the live map). All four are manager-only, all read-only, no migration. `pings/latest` is this codebase's first SQL window-function query (`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY rep_id ORDER BY recorded_at DESC)`) and also its first query shaped as a subquery+`aliased()` rather than a plain `select(Model)` - genuinely novel enough that tenant isolation on it needed real proof, not assumption, since it returns live rep location data. Verified twice: the implementer added a real end-to-end cross-tenant test through the actual client/JWT path, and the task reviewer independently reproduced that AND compiled the generated SQL, confirming the ambient tenancy filter (`with_loader_criteria`) applies both inside the window-function subquery and on the outer alias - safe by construction, not by luck. Per-task review also caught a real gap in the plan's own test code: `test_manager_lists_zone_assignments` asserted an empty list without ever creating a real assignment, so it would have passed against a stub `return []` - fixed by actually assigning a rep first and asserting the specific row comes back. A claimed cross-tenant leak on that same endpoint (raised by the fix's own implementer) was investigated and disproven with a live throwaway test - the ambient filter already covers it, exactly like every other list endpoint in this codebase. 246/246 tests passing, ruff clean. Backend surface for Phase 7 is now complete; next: write