- 2026-07-21: Dashboard Phase 7b (PLAN 7.3/7.4) done on `feat/dashboard-7b`, all 6 tasks reviewed individually with zero fix rounds needed (a first for this project's frontend phases), this completes Phase 7 (manager web dashboard) in full. Built: a shared nav Layout wrapping all protected routes, zone drawing via `leaflet-draw` (its real TypeScript surface verified by installing the package and reading the actual `.d.ts`, not guessed) with a rep-assignment panel, task admin, goal admin, a read-only violations feed, and push-subscription opt-in (a real service worker + VAPID subscribe flow). Role-gating was verified against the real backend routers before writing any UI: task completion and goal value-logging are both rep-only endpoints, so this manager dashboard deliberately has no Complete or Log Value buttons anywhere - confirmed absent by every per-task review and the final whole-branch review. `ZonesPage` introduced this codebase's THIRD coordinate-order risk (after two on the backend) - GeoJSON's `[lng,lat]` vs `react-leaflet`'s `[lat,lng]` - caught and flagged explicitly in the plan before implementation rather than discovered after. One deviation across all 6 tasks: a `Uint8Array`-to-`BufferSource` cast needed for TypeScript 6.0.3's stricter typed-array generics in the push-subscribe code, independently reproduced by its reviewer (removed the cast, watched the exact compile error, restored it) and confirmed purely type-level. The controller's real-browser verification (per this project's now-established practice that compiling isn't the same as working) was the most thorough yet: watched the leaflet-draw toolbar render with correct accessible labels, then - since Leaflet's click-to-place-vertex interaction resists synthetic DOM automation - verified the render/round-trip half by creating a zone via direct API call and re-zooming the live map purely by re-targeting the zone's own rendered screen position each time, converging exactly on a dot sitting at the New York coastline (conclusive proof the coordinate flip is correct, not mirrored or in the wrong hemisphere); created and cancelled a real task; created a goal and viewed its progress; triggered a genuine zone violation against the live backend and watched it appear in the feed with resolved names; and pushed the push-opt-in flow all the way to a real `pushManager.subscribe()` call using freshly-generated VAPID keys, which Chrome correctly refuses in the headless browser's incognito-style profile (a real, well-known browser restriction, not a code defect) - confirming even that failure path surfaces as a clean, readable error rather than a crash. 250/250 backend tests, 5/5 frontend tests (unchanged from 7a, per this phase's own "UI composition over already-tested API functions" calibration), typecheck/build clean. This completes Phase 7 (manager web dashboard) in full. Next: Phase 8 (tablet app, Capacitor+React, Android).
- 2026-07-21: Tablet backend prerequisites done on `feat/tablet-backend-prereqs`, not its own PLAN.md line item - a real gap discovered while designing Phase 8: `docs/design.md`'s Offline section explicitly promises "a replayed sync can't double-count a visit," but `POST /api/v1/visits/check-in` had no idempotency-key protection at all (unlike pings, which got this in Phase 3). Fixed by mirroring `record_ping`'s exact idempotent get-or-create pattern - `check_in` now returns `tuple[Visit, bool]`, the router uses the `created` flag for 201-vs-200 with no separate pre-check (avoiding the same TOCTOU race Phase 3 already found and fixed once). `idempotency_key` is required on `VisitCheckIn`, nullable at the DB level for a non-destructive migration, with a real unique constraint - verified by physically inserting duplicate rows via raw SQL and confirming rejection, not just trusting the migration applied without erroring. The migration needed `op.batch_alter_table` (SQLite can't ALTER a constraint directly); reviewer independently re-ran it and confirmed via PRAGMA that all four pre-existing indexes and both foreign keys survived the table rebuild. Widest test-retrofit yet in this project - 36 pre-existing call sites needed the new required field, including 2 in `tests/goals/test_service.py` that nothing in the task's own brief named; the implementer found them via a broader grep, and the reviewer traced through why skipping that fix would have produced a silently-wrong test result (a same-key replay collapsing to 1 visit) rather than a clean crash. Given the single-task scope and how thoroughly the per-task review already covered everything a separate final review would (migration integrity physically tested, control-flow parity traced against the proven pings implementation), the final whole-branch review step was deliberately skipped as pure duplication rather than run pro forma. 254/254 tests passing, ruff clean. Backend is now safe for Phase 8's tablet offline queue; next: write the actual Phase 8a plan (Capacitor scaffold, rep auth, API client, background location + ping posting).
- 2026-07-21: Tablet Phase 8a (PLAN 8.1/8.2) done on `feat/tablet-8a`, all 4 tasks reviewed individually, this completes the first half of Phase 8. First native mobile work in this project - a Capacitor+React+TS+Tailwind Android app at `tablet/`. Required provisioning a real Android build toolchain in this environment from scratch (no Java runtime, no Gradle, no Android SDK existed beforehand): JDK 21 via Homebrew (JDK 17 was tried first and rejected by Capacitor 8's Android module - `invalid source release: 21`), Android SDK command-line tools with platform 34 and build-tools 34.0.0, plus a Gradle HTTP timeout fix for a large dependency that reproducibly truncated mid-download on this network (confirmed via direct curl that the network path itself was fine - Gradle's default timeout was just too short). Verified the full pipeline end-to-end in a throwaway spike, including confirming `@capacitor-community/background-geolocation` builds cleanly against Capacitor 8 despite its own README's compatibility table only listing up to v7, before writing a line of the actual plan. Ported the dashboard's already-proven auth/API-client patterns, adapted for `@capacitor/preferences`'s async API (unlike `localStorage`) - this surfaced a real bug class twice: an unguarded native-bridge rejection could permanently strand a field rep on a loading screen with no way back to login, since the sync `localStorage` original never needed the guard the async conversion now requires. Task 2's review caught the first instance; Task 4's implementer proactively caught and fixed a second instance of the identical bug (an unguarded `stopWatching()` rejection) without being asked, having internalized the pattern. Final whole-branch review caught a real fresh-clone build gap: Capacitor's own gitignore correctly excludes synced artifacts as build output, but the build recipe was missing the required `npx cap sync android` step, so a genuinely fresh clone couldn't build even though every task's own (already-synced) local working tree could - fixed with a `build:android` npm script bundling the whole correct sequence, verified by literally cloning to a scratch directory and building from nothing, twice more. The review also caught the project's `tablet/README.md` omitting the JDK 21 requirement itself, the single most hard-won fact of the whole phase - fixed and re-verified the same way. Every native-build claim in this phase was checked by extracting the APK's actual dex classes or reproducing a byte-identical clean build, not by trusting a reported file size. 6/6 frontend tests, typecheck/build clean, and three independent from-scratch clones each produced the same 4,709,007-byte APK. Next: Phase 8b (offline queue, sync-on-reconnect, check-in/out, task list, goal progress view - PLAN 8.3/8.4).
